= Chapter 2

== 2.1 Introduction
- Managers are confused for what they should do; should they focus on making firms more tech-y or stay with tradition.
- As a manager, you must have the ability to size up a firm's strategic position and understand the likelihood of sustainability 

== 2.2 Powerful Resources
*Imitation-Resistant Value Chains* 
- There are several resources that firms can use to improve. Any of them can be used by themselves, but it is much better when multiple are utilized together.
- There are *imitation-resistant value chains*, which are business strategies that are unique and difficult to replicate. 
  - Technologies play an enabling role in this aspect. 
- *Value chain* is a set of interrelated acitivites that bring products or services to market. 

*The Value Chain Framework*
- 5 primary components of the value chain:
  - _Inbound logistics_: getting needed materials and inputs into the firm from the suppliers.
  - _Operations_: turning the inputs into products and services.
  - _Outbound logistics_: delivering the products/services to consumers, retails, partners, etc.
  - _Marketing/Sales_: engaging with customers, setting prices, promotions, and transactions.
  - _Support_: maintaining servies, helping with customer supports, etc.
- There are also some secondary components:
  - _Firm infrastructure_: general management, planning, info systems, finance, functions that help the whole firm.
  - _Human resource management_: recruiting, hiring, training, and development.
  - _Technology/resource and development_: innovation, new product and process design.
  - _Procurement_: sourcing and purchasing functions.
- The components are not in order. Ex: an order from marketing can trigger an inbound logistic.
- If a firm's value chain cannot be easily copied, or if the value chain can create and strengthen other strategic assets over time, then it is an important source of competitive advantage.
- The value chain can also reveal weaknesses, which is where technologies can help. 
  - *Supply chain management (SCM)*: linking inbound and outbound logistics with operations.
  - *Customer relationship management (CRM)*: supporting sales, marketing, and R&D in some cases.
  - *Enterprise resource planning (ERP)*: software implemented in modules to automate the entire value chain.
- These software tools can also be purchased by competitors for their own value chain, so it does not have as much of a competitive advantage. 
- Using third party SCM, CRM, and ERP software usually means needing a specific way of doing things. 
  - Ex: Dell stopped logistics and manufacturing modules of a packaged ERP implementation because it would mean the whole firm making big changes that would make Dell do the same thing as its competitors.
  - Ex2: On the other hand, Apple has no problem using third party ERP software because the firm competes on product uniqueness, not operational differences.

*Looking at: Dell*
- Dell had a superefficient, vertically integrated manufacturing and direct-to-consumer model (sell directly to consumers, not to retailers) that helped the firm earn 7x more profit compared to rival PC firms.
- It was a model that was resistant to imitation; rivals had to share a cut of sales with less efficient retail chains for the majority of their sales. Any retail could just go to a separate supplier for similar product. 
- After 2 decades, rivals started to improve their manufacturing efficiency. 
- Cost of computing also fell, which means that the price advantage that Dell always had over competitors did not mean much.
- The direct-to-consumer model suffered when notebook PCs were more commoditized than desktop. 
  - It is easier to test them out, type on them, lift them, etc. This means it is easier to compare products. 
- Over time, rivals took over the spot that Dell had. It even abandoned its direct business model and sells to third party retail. 

== 2.3 Barriers to Entry, Technology, and Timing
- The barrier to entry for tech-centri business is not that high. Anyone can build anything in the Internet. 
- Do not "follow", always try to stay ahead. 
  - Blockbuster waited while Netflix pioneered, resulting in Blockbuster losing #sym.dollar\1 billion while Netflix's profits went up 7x. 
  - Sotheby's dimissed eBay, resulting in Sotheby's losing #sym.dollar\6 million while eBay earned #sym.dollar\2.4 billion in profits.
  - Barnes & Noble waited 17 months to respond to Amazon, resulting Amazon gaining 12x the profits.
- Time lead and technology are competitive advantages when the firms are able to utilize it well. 
- Strategic positioning means that a firm has differences that cannot be matched by rivals. Moving first is advantageous when the time lead is used to create resources that are valuable, had to imitate, and rare. 
- Most tech can be copied, but the lead that tech-enabled firms had was used to create network effects, switching costs, data assets, and help build solid and well-respected brands. 

#let yahoo = "Yahoo!"
*Looking at: Google and Yahoo*
- #yahoo maintained its lead in email because the firm matched and nullified Gmail's most significatn innovations before Google could do much.
  - The firm's failure to respond to the emergence of Google as a threat in search advertising gave them the time it needed to build a profitable Internet firm.
- Seach is seen as a commodity. #yahoo saw no conflict in taking an early investment stake in Google or in using the firm for its search results.
- However, #yahoo did not pay attention to Google's advance. Google's innovations in technology and interface were unmatched over time, allowing it to build its brand, scale, and advertising network that grew from network effects whereby content providers and advertisers attract one another.
- Google's rise to power while being late to the search party is not a sign of power, but rather a story about the failure of incumbents to monitor their competitive landscape, recognize new rivals, and react to challenges. 

== 2.4 Key Framework - The Five Forces of Industry Competitive Advantage
- "The dirty little secret of the strategy industry is that is doesn't have any theory of strategy creation" - Hamel, 1997.
- There are no easy method for strategy creation, but frameworks can help give an example or foundation. 
- One of the most popular frameworks for examining a firm's competitive environment is *Porter's five forces*.
  - Also known as the Industry and Competitive Analysis.
  - Porter says that studying these forces will show an industry's fundamental attractiveness, expose the uderlying drivers of the average industry profitability, and gives insight into how profitability will evolve in the future.
  - The 5 forces:
    1. The intensity of rivalry among existing competitors
    2. The threat of new entrants
    3. The threat of substitute goods or services
    4. The bargaining power of buyers 
    5. The bargaining power of suppliers 
- New technology can shock an industry. 
  - Ex: The rise of the Internet has impacted the forces for music retailers. Retailers realise that customers were buying music online rather than in person. This is an _intensity of rivalry_ because they have to compete online as well as their physical location.
  - There are also _new entrants_ with the previous example with firms such as Amazon showing up in music retails. 
  - There are also _substitutes_ in the music retail industry since CDs are not the only way of consuming music now. 
  - Consumers also has _bargaining power_ with the shift to online music. The creation of iPods and ITunes means consumers can have thousands of songs in their pockets instead of carrying CDs. Also, theft becomes more prevalent with the boom of online music sharing. Consumers now get to bargain for cheaper music prices, leveraging their bargaining power. 
  - The _bargaining power of suppliers_ also increased. Many music retail chains are in bankruptcy, which means labels have a freer hand in experimenting and bands have new ways to reach fans besides retail. 
- Technology can play a big role in the 5 forces, but it is not the only thing that can cause an industry shock.
  - Government deregulation/intervention, political shock, social and demographic changes.
